    "findingId": "F3",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "adjustedSeverity": "MEDIUM",
    "reasoning": "The primary claim is confirmed by the repository's own test. `packages/mochi/src/staticDirs.test.ts` writes `.env` containing `SECRET=1` into the mounted tree (L14) and then asserts `GET /assets/.env` returns **200** (L101-104), with the comment 'Documented, not desired: Bun's directory route serves everything under the directory' — so the maintainers have empirically pinned the behavior and consider it a wart. `resolveStaticDirs` applies no content policy at all (only prefix shape, root-mount, metachar, assetPrefix, duplicate and stat checks), and `registerStaticDirRoutes` hands the directory to Bun as a native `{ dir }` value (staticDirs.ts:57), where no dotfile notion exists. The divergence from the framework's own twice-implemented policy is real: `isExcludedDotPath` filters at scan time in publicDir.ts:14-27/54-58 (tests at publicDir.test.ts:36-56 assert `.env` and `.git/config` are skipped and only `.well-known` is allowed), and `Mochi.file` repeats the identical check at Mochi.ts:1480-1485 with a comment explicitly saying it matches the public-dir policy. I also verified the docs gap: the only warning callout for `staticDirs` (docs/160-serve-options.md:105-111) covers the request-pipeline bypass and says nothing about dotfiles, and grepping all of `packages/docs` turns up no dotfile statement for mounts. Traversal itself is genuinely handled (test L113-121 shows `..`, `%2E%2E` and `%2F` all 404), so the finding correctly scopes the issue to canonical dotfile segments. The feature is unreleased (`VersionNote since="0.10.0"`), and it is aimed at exactly the trees most likely to hold a stray `.env` or a `.git` checkout, so the secure-default is worth adding before ship. One correction: the secondary symlink claim is overbroad — Bun's routing docs state the `openat2(RESOLVE_IN_ROOT)` rules are applied by Bun itself on macOS, FreeBSD, and on Linux without `openat2`; only on Windows does Bun 'follow symlinks wherever they lead'. The case-sensitivity caveat is accurate and is documented by Bun. Neither correction touches the dotfile finding."
