    "reasoning": "Confirmed, including the runtime semantics the bug depends on. `applyResolveOptions` rebuilds the header set with `filtered.set(name, value)` while iterating `result.headers.entries()` (hooks.ts:185-197). Bun's own release notes settle that iteration yields separate `set-cookie` entries rather than comma-merging them — v0.5.9 ships the exact test `new Headers([['Set-Cookie','foo=bar']])` + `append('set-Cookie','foo=baz')` → `[...headers]` equals two `['set-cookie', …]` pairs (and v0.8.0 'Fix iterating headers with set-cookie'). So `.set()` overwrites each prior cookie and only the last survives; note the bug holds even under the other possible semantics, since a comma-merged `set-cookie` value re-`set` as one header also loses every cookie after the first at the browser's parser. The framework definitely produces multiple headers: `finalizeCookieHeaders` uses `headers.append('Set-Cookie', …)` per cookie (cookies.ts:143-145). Reachability is confirmed at every claimed site and one more: Mochi.ts:1905 finalizes the jar *before* the filter on the protected fetch-fallback path; Mochi.ts:1907-1908 passes the user's `fetch` Response straight in; Mochi.ts:1128-1130 does the same for a form action's own Response; errors.ts:74-75 for a `handleError` Response; and Mochi.ts:1226-1227 for **every** `Mochi.api` handler's Response — an API route that appends a session cookie and a CSRF cookie silently ships only the second. Page/API routes using `ctx.cookies` are spared only by ordering (finalize at Mochi.ts:993 / 1242, after the middleware chain), which is luck, not design. The repo already owns the correct primitive — `collectHeaderPairs()` (utils/index.ts:102-115) exists precisely because `getSetCookie()` keeps the values separate — and `applyResolveOptions` simply doesn't use it. I verified the coverage claim: grepping all `*.test.ts` in the monorepo returns zero hits for `filterResponseHeaders`, `transformPage`, or `applyResolveOptions`, and no in-repo app uses `filterResponseHeaders` (support/src/embedHeaders.ts only mentions it in a comment explaining why it rebuilds the Response instead). Silent loss of `Set-Cookie` on a documented, example-ed middleware feature justifies HIGH_BUG."
  },
