    "findingId": "F5",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "adjustedSeverity": "BUG",
    "reasoning": "Confirmed as a real latent defect, distinct from F4 (different lines, different mechanism — entity-header/body divergence rather than Set-Cookie collapse), so not a duplicate. hooks.ts:176-181 constructs the transformed Response with `new Headers(result.headers)`, carrying every entity header across a body rewrite. The Content-Length half hinges on whether Bun honors an explicit value, and it does: Bun v1.1.43 added 'Override Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding in Bun.serve()' with the example `new Response('Hello World', { headers: { 'Content-Length': '7' } })`, and Mochi itself depends on that behavior for HEAD at Mochi.ts:1492-1493. The trigger is plausible: `new Response(Bun.file('page.html'))` populates Content-Length (Mochi.ts:1495 states this, and Bun's routing docs confirm automatic Content-Length/Content-Range for file bodies), its type is `text/html` so it enters the transform branch, and such a Response reaches `applyResolveOptions` from a `fetch` fallback (Mochi.ts:1908), a form action returning its own Response (1130), a `handleError` Response (errors.ts:75), or an API route (1227) — shipping a declared length that no longer matches the bytes. The ETag/Last-Modified half is unambiguous in code and matters most for `transformPage`'s documented purpose: docs/125-transform-page.md:57 says to use it for request-dependent values only (nonces, A/B classes), so a per-request transform under an entity validator computed for the untransformed file is exactly the shape a shared cache mishandles. `transformPage` is live in all three deployed apps (shared/src/index.ts:23-29 analytics, site/src/index.ts:67 and :100, site/src/shot/routes.ts:33), though no in-repo handler currently returns an HTML Response carrying these headers, so the bug is latent rather than firing today. Two notes on the write-up: the in-repo precedents are real but only half-consistent — compress.ts:62-64 does copy headers and explicitly `delete('Content-Length')` because the body changed, and appendDebugTail bails on `Content-Encoding` (Mochi.ts:186-188), yet appendDebugTail then appends to the body while reusing `response.headers` verbatim (L198-202), i.e. it has the same Content-Length gap. And the Content-Encoding rationale ('result.text() has already decoded the body') only holds for a Response obtained from `fetch()`; for a locally-constructed pre-compressed body `.text()` does not decode, in which case the transform corrupts the compressed bytes instead. Either way transforming an encoded body is wrong. Confirmed zero test coverage for `transformPage` anywhere in the monorepo."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F6",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "adjustedSeverity": "BUG",
    "reasoning": "Every step checks out. `publicRouteKey` is bare `encodeURI(urlPath)` (publicDir.ts:35-37), and `encodeURI` by specification leaves `:`, `*`, `?`, `#` unescaped; publicDir.test.ts:86-90 deliberately pins that under-encoding to keep `,()` literal, and nothing re-escapes afterwards. The result is used directly as a Bun route-pattern key at publicDir.ts:88/95, and Bun's routing docs define the pattern grammar and precedence as exact > `:param` > `/*` > global `/*`, so a public file named `:id` registers `/:id` — matching every single-segment URL no declared exact route claims — and one named `*` registers the global catch-all, which is precisely the failure staticDirs.ts:17-21 refuses a root mount to avoid ('answers every unmatched request with Bun's own 404 — shadowing the framework's error page, `fetch` fallback and asset routes'). The framework demonstrably knows these two characters are pattern syntax: `isWarmablePattern` rejects any pattern containing `:` or `*` for exactly this reason (warmup.ts:19-22). Neither guard can catch it, as claimed: the runtime check is the exact-string `routeKey in routes` (publicDir.ts:89), and the build-time collision check is the same exact-string comparison against both the raw and encoded form (cli/build.ts:129-135), so `/:id` never collides with a declared `/login`. Ordering is as described — user routes are registered first and public files are merged into the same `bunRoutes` object afterwards (Mochi.ts:1859). The security framing is the weakest part: `registerPublicRoutes` runs at boot and, in development only, from devWatcher.ts:737, so an upload-written filename needs a restart to take effect, and `?`/`#` surviving merely makes a file unreachable. But the correctness defect is certain and easy to hit innocently — any ISO-8601-timestamped filename contains colons on a POSIX box — and the outcome is either a misrouted URL subtree or a boot-time pattern error. BUG is the right severity; the suggested post-`encodeURI` escaping of `: * ? #` still matches what browsers send for those characters in a path."
  }
]
